-- [Neuron Anatomy & Signal Flow] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher applies a drug that blocks voltage-gated sodium channels to a single, tightly localized region of a multipolar neuron. After treatment, the neuron''s dendrites still develop graded potentials when synapses are activated, and its axon still conducts an action potential normally when one is triggered artificially partway down the fiber. However, the neuron no longer initiates its own action potentials in response to incoming synaptic input, no matter how strong that input is. Which region of the neuron did the drug most likely block?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The axon hillock and initial segment"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The dendritic branches"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The axon terminals"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The nodes of Ranvier"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Information flows through a neuron with a fixed polarity: dendrites and the cell body receive and sum graded potentials, and if the summed voltage reaches threshold at the trigger zone, an all-or-none action potential is initiated and then propagated down the axon to the terminals. OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e states that the axon hillock is a tapering of the cell body that narrows into the initial segment, and that "action potentials are generated in the trigger zone, which is a combination of the axon hillock and initial segment." The experiment isolates the initiation step: reception (dendrites) and conduction (axon) are intact, but the neuron cannot start an action potential of its own. (Choice A) is correct: the axon hillock and initial segment form the trigger zone, the region with the density of voltage-gated sodium channels where summed graded potentials are converted into an action potential; blocking those channels here abolishes initiation while leaving reception and downstream conduction untouched. (Choice B) is incorrect: dendrites receive input and produce graded potentials, which the scenario says are still intact, so a dendritic block would not selectively eliminate action-potential initiation. (Choice C) is incorrect: axon terminals are the output end where the action potential triggers neurotransmitter release onto the target cell; blocking them would impair signaling to other cells, not the neuron''s own ability to fire. (Choice D) is incorrect: the nodes of Ranvier are gaps in the myelin sheath that speed propagation along the axon, but the scenario states axonal conduction is still normal, so they are not the site of initiation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of neuronal signal flow to deduce which region generates the action potential from the pattern of preserved and lost functions.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '544a32e9-5580-45ba-a8cc-4bceda653aa7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '544a32e9-5580-45ba-a8cc-4bceda653aa7';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('544a32e9-5580-45ba-a8cc-4bceda653aa7', 'B', 'misconception', 'action potentials begin where input is received'),
  ('544a32e9-5580-45ba-a8cc-4bceda653aa7', 'C', 'misconception', 'the output/terminal end starts the signal'),
  ('544a32e9-5580-45ba-a8cc-4bceda653aa7', 'D', 'misconception', 'propagation gaps are where the impulse originates');

-- [Myelin & Myelinating Glia] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In multiple sclerosis, the immune system strips myelin from central axons while leaving the axonal membrane, its ion pumps, and its voltage-gated Na+ channels intact. A neurologist records from a single demyelinated axon and finds that action potentials still occur but travel far more slowly than before. Which change best explains the slowed conduction along the affected axon?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Local current now leaks across the internodal membrane, so depolarization can no longer spread efficiently to the next node."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The loss of myelin removes the axon''s only source of ATP, so the Na+/K+ pump can no longer restore the resting potential between spikes."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Voltage-gated Na+ channels are destroyed along with the myelin, forcing the signal to travel by passive spread alone."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Without myelin the axon reverses its polarity, so each action potential must now propagate backward toward the cell body."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' In a myelinated axon, voltage-gated Na+ channels are clustered only at the nodes of Ranvier; between nodes the myelin acts as insulation so that the inward Na+ current spreads passively down the internode and still arrives above threshold at the next node, producing fast saltatory ("leaping") conduction. The textbook notes that when myelin insulation is compromised, "electrical signaling [becomes] slower," which is why demyelinating diseases like MS impair conduction even when the axon and its channels survive. (Choice A) is correct: myelin''s job is to keep the internodal membrane sealed so charge does not dissipate before reaching the next node; stripping it lets local current leak out, the depolarization falls off before the next node, and the axon reverts toward slow continuous conduction. (Choice B) is incorrect: myelin is glial-derived insulation, not a metabolic fuel source, and the axon''s mitochondria and Na+/K+ pumps are unaffected by demyelination, so ATP supply and repolarization are not the bottleneck. (Choice C) is incorrect: the stem specifies the voltage-gated Na+ channels remain intact, and it is the loss of insulation—not loss of channels—that slows the signal; the channels are still present and can still fire at the nodes. (Choice D) is incorrect: myelin does not set the direction of propagation; the action potential still travels toward the axon terminals because the membrane behind it is refractory, regardless of whether myelin is present. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the insulating role of myelin in saltatory conduction to a novel demyelination scenario and predict the biophysical cause of slowed signaling.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '01b37a48-8ac2-4952-9962-20344ef1677b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '01b37a48-8ac2-4952-9962-20344ef1677b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('01b37a48-8ac2-4952-9962-20344ef1677b', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-mechanism'),
  ('01b37a48-8ac2-4952-9962-20344ef1677b', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses-insulation-with-channels'),
  ('01b37a48-8ac2-4952-9962-20344ef1677b', 'D', 'misconception', 'wrong-direction-of-propagation');

-- [CNS Tissue Organization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During an ischemic stroke, neurons in the affected region of the brain fire repeatedly and then fail, causing potassium ions to accumulate to abnormally high levels in the extracellular fluid surrounding them. A neuroscientist wants to identify the glial cell that normally counteracts this buildup by taking up excess extracellular potassium and helping restore the ionic environment neurons need to signal. Which cell fills this role?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The astrocyte, which regulates the concentration of ions in the extracellular space around CNS neurons"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The oligodendrocyte, which wraps insulating myelin around axon segments of CNS neurons"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The microglial cell, which ingests damaged cells and pathogens throughout CNS tissue"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The ependymal cell, which filters blood to produce the cerebrospinal fluid that bathes the CNS"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' The four glial cells of the central nervous system each perform a distinct supporting role, and answering requires matching the described task—buffering extracellular ions—to the correct cell rather than recognizing a named structure. OpenStax A&P 2e states that astrocytes support CNS neurons by "maintaining the concentration of chemicals in the extracellular space" and that following an ischemic event in which "extracellular K+ levels are elevated," the "astrocytes in the area are equipped to clear excess K+ to aid the pump." Because the stimulus describes potassium accumulating outside neurons and asks which cell takes it up to restore the ionic environment, the answer is the cell whose defined job is managing extracellular ion concentrations. (Choice A) is correct: the astrocyte is the CNS glial cell that maintains the extracellular chemical environment, including clearing excess K+ after neuronal activity, so it is the cell that counteracts the potassium buildup. (Choice B) is incorrect: the oligodendrocyte insulates CNS axons with myelin, which speeds signal conduction but does nothing to remove ions from the extracellular fluid. (Choice C) is incorrect: microglia act as resident macrophages that phagocytose damaged cells and pathogens; they perform immune surveillance rather than ionic buffering, though tissue damage from a stroke could later recruit them. (Choice D) is incorrect: ependymal cells line the ventricles and produce cerebrospinal fluid at the choroid plexus, a bulk-fluid function distinct from locally regulating extracellular ion levels next to signaling neurons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct functional roles of the CNS glial cell types to a novel physiological scenario and infer which cell restores the extracellular ionic balance.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing the insulating/myelinating glial cell with the ion-buffering one'),
  ('43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'invoking the immune glial cell because the scenario involves tissue damage'),
  ('43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'citing a real CNS glial function (CSF production) that does not address local extracellular ions');

-- [Neuron Functional Types] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A histologist examines a single neuron isolated from a peripheral nerve. A lone process emerges from the cell body and shortly divides into two branches. One branch ends in fine endings embedded in the skin, where it responds to mechanical deformation; the other branch travels toward the spinal cord and forms synapses within the central nervous system. The cell body itself sits in a ganglion just outside the cord and lies along the pathway rather than at either end of it. Based on this architecture, the neuron is best classified by function as:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A sensory (afferent) neuron"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A motor (efferent) neuron"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An interneuron (association neuron)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A postganglionic autonomic motor neuron"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' Neurons can be grouped by their structural shape (unipolar, bipolar, multipolar) or by their function (sensory/afferent, motor/efferent, interneuron), and the two schemes are correlated: the described cell has a single process that splits into two branches, with its cell body positioned along the pathway inside a ganglion, which is the signature of a unipolar (pseudo-unipolar) neuron. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that in these cells the peripheral branch bears dendritic endings that receive the stimulus and the other branch "projects... into the central nervous system," and critically that "unipolar cells are exclusively sensory neurons." The key inference is directional and structural: information flows from a peripheral stimulus toward the CNS, and only the sensory class has this pseudo-unipolar architecture. (Choice A) is correct: dendritic endings in the skin detect the stimulus and the axon carries the signal inward to the CNS, which is the afferent (sensory) direction, and the single-process, ganglion-seated shape is unique to sensory neurons. (Choice B) is incorrect: a motor (efferent) neuron carries commands outward from the CNS to an effector, the opposite direction from the described periphery-to-CNS flow, and motor neurons are multipolar rather than pseudo-unipolar. (Choice C) is incorrect: an interneuron connects neurons entirely within the CNS and does not extend a process out to receive a peripheral stimulus, so a cell with endings in the skin and a body in a peripheral ganglion cannot be an interneuron. (Choice D) is incorrect: a postganglionic autonomic motor neuron does sit in a peripheral ganglion, but it is efferent—its axon projects away from the ganglion to a visceral effector such as a gland or smooth muscle—whereas this cell''s axon projects into the CNS and its dendrites sense a stimulus. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structure-to-function correlation of neuron classification—reading the single bifurcating process, the ganglionic cell body, and the periphery-to-CNS direction of signal flow—to infer the functional class rather than recall a definition directly.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a713b712-af90-4ec8-97c3-69b2217dc0d5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a713b712-af90-4ec8-97c3-69b2217dc0d5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a713b712-af90-4ec8-97c3-69b2217dc0d5', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confusing afferent (into CNS) with efferent (out of CNS) direction of flow'),
  ('a713b712-af90-4ec8-97c3-69b2217dc0d5', 'C', 'misconception', 'treating any relay neuron as an interneuron regardless of CNS-only location'),
  ('a713b712-af90-4ec8-97c3-69b2217dc0d5', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'over-weighting the ganglion location cue and assuming a peripheral-ganglion cell must be autonomic/efferent');

-- [Neuron Anatomy & Signal Flow] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher stimulates several dendrites of a multipolar neuron and records where the action potential first arises. Even when a dendrite receives an unusually strong depolarizing input, the impulse reliably originates at the axon hillock rather than near the stimulated dendrite. Which membrane property of the axon hillock best accounts for its consistently being the site of impulse initiation?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It has the highest density of voltage-gated sodium channels of any region of the neuron"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It is the most heavily myelinated region of the neuron"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It has the highest density of ligand-gated neurotransmitter receptors of any region of the neuron"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It has the highest density of sodium-potassium pumps of any region of the neuron"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Graded potentials from many synapses spread passively toward the axon hillock and initial segment, where they summate; an action potential fires only where the membrane can reach threshold and rapidly regenerate the depolarization. According to OpenStax A&P 2e, graded potentials "summate at a specific location at the beginning of the axon to initiate the action potential, namely the initial segment," and "the axon hillock is essentially the initial segment of the axon," a region that has "a high density of voltage-gated Na+ channels that initiate the depolarizing phase of the action potential." (Choice A) is correct: because voltage-gated sodium channels are packed most densely here, this region has the lowest threshold, so summed inputs trigger the regenerative sodium influx of the action potential at the hillock before they can do so anywhere else. (Choice B) is incorrect: the axon hillock is not the most heavily myelinated region—it is unmyelinated, and myelin insulates the axon between nodes of Ranvier downstream; insulation would not lower threshold or explain why the impulse begins at the hillock. (Choice C) is incorrect: ligand-gated receptors are concentrated at the postsynaptic membranes of dendrites and the soma, where they produce graded potentials; they do not generate the all-or-none action potential and are not enriched at the hillock. (Choice D) is incorrect: the sodium-potassium pump restores resting gradients over time and does not open in response to depolarization, so a high pump density cannot account for rapid, threshold-triggered impulse initiation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between voltage-gated sodium channel density and firing threshold to explain why the action potential originates at the axon hillock rather than at a more strongly stimulated dendrite.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'dc9aaeee-a329-4db1-93d6-8f61f958e388';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'dc9aaeee-a329-4db1-93d6-8f61f958e388';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('dc9aaeee-a329-4db1-93d6-8f61f958e388', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflating the myelinated axon with the impulse-initiation site'),
  ('dc9aaeee-a329-4db1-93d6-8f61f958e388', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributing impulse initiation to the input (ligand-gated) receptors that produce graded potentials'),
  ('dc9aaeee-a329-4db1-93d6-8f61f958e388', 'D', 'misconception', 'confusing the resting-gradient Na+/K+ pump with the voltage-gated channels that fire the action potential');

-- [Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia] ans=D hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A histologist examines CNS tissue from a patient and finds that one glial population, which normally forms a single ciliated layer lining the ventricles and wraps around the blood capillaries of the choroid plexus, has been selectively destroyed. Following this loss, the clear ventricular fluid — normally a filtrate of blood containing water, electrolytes, and small molecules — is no longer generated. Which glial cell was lost, and what specific role does its position at the capillary wall serve?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Astrocytes, which extend processes onto capillaries to help regulate ion concentrations in the extracellular fluid."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Microglia, which contact capillaries to intercept and phagocytose pathogens entering the nervous tissue."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Oligodendrocytes, which reach out to capillaries to wrap nearby central axons in insulating myelin."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ependymal cells, which surround capillaries to filter blood into a fluid that circulates through the CNS."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' The four CNS glia each occupy characteristic positions and perform distinct jobs, and the diagnostic clue here is a cell that lines the ventricles, bears cilia, and physically wraps the capillaries of the choroid plexus. OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e states that at the choroid plexus, ependymal cells "surround blood capillaries and filter the blood to make CSF," a fluid that is "essentially water, small molecules, and electrolytes," and that these cells "line each ventricle" and have "cilia on their apical surface." (Choice A) is incorrect: astrocytes do send processes onto capillaries and do "maintain the concentration of chemicals in the extracellular space," but their capillary contact contributes to the blood-brain barrier and ion buffering, not to filtering blood into ventricular fluid. (Choice B) is incorrect: microglia are the CNS-resident macrophages that "ingest and digest" diseased cells and pathogens, so although immune surveillance is real, it does not generate a blood-derived fluid. (Choice C) is incorrect: oligodendrocytes reach out to axon segments to "insulate it in myelin," not to capillaries, so myelination is unrelated to producing ventricular fluid. (Choice D) is correct: only ependymal cells occupy the choroid plexus, wrap its capillaries, and filter blood to produce the clear fluid that circulates through the CNS, so their loss halts production of that fluid. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural and positional clues (ventricular lining, cilia, capillary-wrapping at the choroid plexus) to identify the glial cell whose blood-filtering function is described, rather than recalling a stated cushioning role.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct-function-wrong-outcome'),
  ('6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct-function-wrong-outcome'),
  ('6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25', 'C', 'misconception', 'role-confusion');

-- [Neuron Anatomy & Signal Flow] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher records from a multipolar neuron and confirms that a normal action potential is still generated at its trigger zone in response to synaptic input. However, a drug applied to one region of the same neuron prevents that action potential from ever reaching and exciting the downstream target cell, even though neurotransmitter release machinery and the target''s receptors are intact. At which structure is the drug most likely acting?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The dendrites"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cell body (soma)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The axon"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The postsynaptic membrane of the target cell"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Information flows through a neuron in one direction—from the dendrites, across the cell body, and down the axon—giving the neuron its polarity; the action potential is generated at the trigger zone (axon hillock plus initial segment) and then propagates along the axon to the axon terminals and synaptic end bulbs, where it triggers neurotransmitter release onto the target. The scenario fixes the problem downstream of impulse generation but upstream of the target: the action potential is made normally, yet it never arrives to excite the next cell, and both the release machinery and the target receptors are known to be intact. (Choice A) is incorrect: the dendrites lie on the input side of the neuron, receiving synaptic signals and carrying them inward to the cell body; blocking them would prevent input, but here the action potential is already being generated normally. (Choice B) is incorrect: the cell body sits before the trigger zone in the signal path, so a lesion there would impair generation of the impulse, yet the stem states the action potential is produced normally at the trigger zone. (Choice C) is correct: the axon is the conducting output fiber that carries the action potential away from the trigger zone toward the terminals, so a drug that blocks propagation along the axon would stop the already-generated impulse from ever reaching the target, exactly matching the observation. (Choice D) is incorrect: the postsynaptic membrane belongs to the target cell and carries its receptors, which the stem specifies are intact, so the failure lies in the presynaptic neuron''s own output pathway rather than at reception. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fixed directional flow of signals through a neuron to locate where an intact impulse could be halted before reaching its target.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0a2c3b3d-8bfb-4bf8-8320-df7270c98473';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0a2c3b3d-8bfb-4bf8-8320-df7270c98473';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0a2c3b3d-8bfb-4bf8-8320-df7270c98473', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'input-side structure confused for output pathway'),
  ('0a2c3b3d-8bfb-4bf8-8320-df7270c98473', 'B', 'misconception', 'placing the block before impulse generation despite normal trigger-zone firing'),
  ('0a2c3b3d-8bfb-4bf8-8320-df7270c98473', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'targeting the postsynaptic cell after stem rules out receptor failure');

-- [PNS: Somatic vs Autonomic] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A hiker rounds a bend and is startled by a snake on the trail. In the next second, several changes occur: her heart pounds faster, the pupils of her eyes widen, her palms begin to sweat, and she instantly jumps backward away from the snake. A physiologist reviewing these four responses wants to identify the one that is produced by the somatic nervous system rather than the autonomic nervous system. Which response should she select?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The acceleration of her heart rate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The widening of her pupils"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The onset of sweating on her palms"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The backward jump away from the snake"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' The peripheral nervous system is divided functionally: the somatic nervous system produces voluntary motor responses by contracting skeletal muscle (including reflexive skeletal-muscle movements), whereas the autonomic nervous system produces involuntary responses by acting on cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glandular tissue to regulate homeostasis. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that "the somatic nervous system causes contraction of skeletal muscles," while "the autonomic nervous system controls cardiac and smooth muscle, as well as glandular tissue," and it specifically notes that a startle response such as leaping back "is a reflex involving skeletal muscle contractions." Sorting each response by its effector tissue, rather than by whether it feels automatic, is the key to this problem. (Choice A) is incorrect: the heart is composed of cardiac muscle, an autonomic effector; the sympathetic division accelerates heart rate during the fight-or-flight response, so this is autonomic, not somatic. (Choice B) is incorrect: pupil diameter is set by smooth muscle of the iris, an autonomic effector, and sympathetic activation dilates the pupils to increase visual information—an autonomic response. (Choice C) is incorrect: sweat glands are glandular tissue controlled by the autonomic system, and their activation during a threat is a sympathetic (autonomic) response, not a somatic one. (Choice D) is correct: jumping backward is a contraction of skeletal muscle, the effector of the somatic nervous system; even though it happens reflexively without a conscious decision, it is a somatic motor response because skeletal muscle is involved. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that somatic output acts on skeletal muscle while autonomic output acts on cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands to classify each novel response by its effector tissue.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '199b981a-682c-4830-9e31-d92fc0c962ce';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '199b981a-682c-4830-9e31-d92fc0c962ce';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('199b981a-682c-4830-9e31-d92fc0c962ce', 'A', 'misconception', 'confusing effector tissue: treating rapid involuntary cardiac change as somatic'),
  ('199b981a-682c-4830-9e31-d92fc0c962ce', 'B', 'misconception', 'assuming a conscious sensory-related change (vision) is somatically controlled'),
  ('199b981a-682c-4830-9e31-d92fc0c962ce', 'C', 'misconception', 'classifying a glandular secretion as somatic because it is body-surface and noticeable');

-- [Neuron Functional Types] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a physical exam, a physician taps the patellar tendon just below the kneecap. The tap briefly stretches the quadriceps, activating a muscle-spindle receptor. The receptor''s neuron carries this signal into the spinal cord and synapses directly onto the neuron whose axon leaves the cord to contract the quadriceps, producing the knee jerk with almost no delay. (A separate collateral branch quiets the antagonist hamstring muscles.) Considering only the direct two-neuron pathway that produces the quadriceps contraction, which functional class of reflex-arc neuron is absent from that pathway?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The sensory (afferent) neuron"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The motor (efferent) neuron"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The interneuron"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The postganglionic autonomic neuron"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' A reflex arc is built from a set of functional neuron classes: sensory (afferent) neurons carry input from receptors into the CNS, motor (efferent) neurons carry output from the CNS to effectors, and interneurons sit entirely within the CNS as the integrating center that connects the afferent and efferent branches. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e defines a reflex arc as a circuit involving "a sensory input and motor output... and an integrating center to connect the two branches," and it distinguishes a monosynaptic reflex, "in which the sensory neuron directly activates the motor neuron," from a polysynaptic reflex such as the hot-stove withdrawal, in which an interneuron is interposed between them. The knee-jerk stretch reflex is monosynaptic: the muscle-spindle sensory neuron "will cause direct contraction of the muscle" by synapsing straight onto the motor neuron, so no integrating relay participates in the direct excitatory limb. (Choice A) is incorrect: the sensory (afferent) neuron is present—it is the muscle-spindle neuron that detects the stretch and carries that input into the spinal cord, forming the first neuron of the two-neuron pathway. (Choice B) is incorrect: the motor (efferent) neuron is present—it is the second neuron whose axon leaves the cord to contract the quadriceps, so removing it would eliminate the contraction the stem describes. (Choice C) is correct: because the sensory neuron synapses directly onto the motor neuron, the arc is monosynaptic and contains no interneuron in the excitatory pathway; the interneuron is the integrating class that would only appear if a relay were interposed between the afferent and efferent branches, as in a polysynaptic reflex. (Choice D) is incorrect: the postganglionic autonomic neuron is not one of the reflex arc''s functional classes at all—it belongs to the visceral (autonomic) efferent pathway that drives smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands; the somatic knee-jerk reflex contracts skeletal muscle and never includes an autonomic neuron regardless of synapse count. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the functional classification of reflex-arc neurons and the monosynaptic-versus-polysynaptic distinction to infer, from the stated direct synapse, which neuron class is missing from the arc.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f62255c8-f053-4943-87e1-f909f8decb25';
