-- [Myelin & Myelinating Glia] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a patient with multiple sclerosis, an autoimmune process strips the myelin from axons in a CNS tract while leaving the axons themselves intact. Compared with a healthy axon, how is the propagation of an action potential along one of these affected axons most likely changed, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Conduction slows because depolarizing current now leaks across the bare membrane between nodes instead of jumping ahead"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Conduction speeds up because the insulating layer that had impeded the flow of ions along the axon is gone"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Conduction is unchanged because voltage-gated sodium channels are clustered only at the nodes regardless of myelin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Conduction fails entirely because losing myelin also removes the voltage-gated sodium channels the axon needs"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' In a healthy myelinated axon, the insulating sheath forces the depolarizing sodium current to spread down the inside of the axon and regenerate the action potential only at the nodes of Ranvier, so the impulse effectively "leaps" from node to node in fast saltatory conduction; the internodal distance is tuned so the membrane is still above threshold when the current reaches the next node. When myelin is lost, the current is no longer contained and dissipates across the now-exposed internodal membrane, so the signal must fall back toward slow continuous conduction and, if it decays below threshold, may fail to reach the next node. (Choice A) is correct: with myelin gone, current leaks across the bare membrane rather than being channeled to the next node, so depolarization dissipates and conduction slows—the text notes that when "the insulation around the axons is destroyed," the result is "making electrical signaling slower." (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the actual role of myelin, treating insulation as a barrier that slows ions; in reality myelin speeds conduction by preventing current leak, so removing it makes conduction slower, not faster. (Choice C) is incorrect: although voltage-gated sodium channels are indeed concentrated at the nodes, saltatory conduction still depends on myelin keeping the internodal current from leaking, so demyelination degrades conduction even with the channel distribution unchanged. (Choice D) is incorrect: the myelin sheath is made by glial cells and is separate from the axon''s own membrane proteins, so stripping myelin does not remove the axon''s voltage-gated sodium channels; the axon can still fire, just less efficiently. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of saltatory conduction to a novel demyelination scenario and predict the functional consequence, rather than recall a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b4371073-4e28-4fd9-90e7-bf3af9f33126';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b4371073-4e28-4fd9-90e7-bf3af9f33126';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b4371073-4e28-4fd9-90e7-bf3af9f33126', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'treats myelin as a physical barrier to ion flow, so removing it should speed conduction'),
  ('b4371073-4e28-4fd9-90e7-bf3af9f33126', 'C', 'misconception', 'knows Na+ channels sit at nodes and concludes myelin is therefore irrelevant to conduction speed'),
  ('b4371073-4e28-4fd9-90e7-bf3af9f33126', 'D', 'misconception', 'conflates loss of the glial myelin sheath with loss of the axon''s own voltage-gated channels');

-- [Neuron Functional Types] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a flexor-withdrawal reflex, a subject rapidly pulls a hand away from a hot surface. Researchers selectively silence one class of neuron in the spinal cord and find that the flexor (biceps) still contracts normally, but the antagonist extensor (triceps) now fails to relax during the reflex, briefly resisting the withdrawal. The silenced cells were confined to the spinal gray matter. Which functional class of neuron did the researchers most likely silence?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Sensory (afferent) neurons carrying pain signals from the skin"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Motor (efferent) neurons driving contraction of the biceps"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Interneurons that inhibit the extensor''s motor neuron"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Motor (efferent) neurons driving contraction of the triceps"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' In a spinal withdrawal reflex, sensory input is integrated within the gray matter before a motor response is produced, and smooth withdrawal requires the antagonist muscle to relax while the agonist contracts. Because the neuromuscular junction is strictly excitatory and skeletal muscle cannot actively relax, relaxation of the antagonist must be produced by inhibiting its motor neuron rather than by any signal sent directly to the muscle. (Choice C) is correct: OpenStax states that during the withdrawal reflex an interneuron in the spinal cord "inhibits the motor neuron that controls the triceps brachii" by hyperpolarizing it, so the triceps relaxes; silencing this integrating interneuron removes the inhibition, leaving the extensor motor neuron active and the triceps unable to relax—exactly the observed deficit. (Choice A) is incorrect: silencing the afferent pain neurons would abolish the trigger for the entire reflex, so the biceps would not contract either, yet the flexor contraction was preserved. (Choice B) is incorrect: the biceps motor neuron drives the flexor contraction that was still normal, so silencing it would weaken withdrawal rather than block antagonist relaxation. (Choice D) is incorrect: silencing the triceps motor neuron would itself let the triceps go slack, which is the opposite of the observed failure to relax, and this cell lies in the ventral horn projecting to muscle rather than acting as the intermediary that produces inhibition. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the roles of sensory, motor, and interneurons to a reflex-arc perturbation and infer which class produces antagonist inhibition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '46be95ca-4d35-49af-af9b-7b726954b606';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '46be95ca-4d35-49af-af9b-7b726954b606';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('46be95ca-4d35-49af-af9b-7b726954b606', 'A', 'misconception', 'wrong-link-in-reflex-arc'),
  ('46be95ca-4d35-49af-af9b-7b726954b606', 'B', 'partial_truth', 'correct-cell-wrong-role'),
  ('46be95ca-4d35-49af-af9b-7b726954b606', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'silencing-produces-opposite-effect');

-- [CNS Tissue Organization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The axons carrying visual information leave the retina as a bundle called the optic nerve. As these axons pass the optic chiasm and travel deeper into the brain toward the thalamus, the same retinal fibers continue on without any being added or removed. In the region beyond the chiasm, where the fibers now lie inside the brain, this bundle of axons is instead designated the optic:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "tract"}, {"label": "B", "text": "ganglion"}, {"label": "C", "text": "nucleus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "nerve"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Anatomical names for neural structures depend jointly on two variables: what the structure is made of (bundled axons versus clustered cell bodies) and where it lies (central nervous system versus peripheral nervous system). A bundle of axons is called a nerve in the PNS but a tract in the CNS, while a cluster of cell bodies is called a ganglion in the PNS but a nucleus in the CNS; because the optic fibers pass from peripheral to central without any change in their physical content, only the location-dependent name changes. (Choice A) is correct: past the optic chiasm the fibers lie within the brain, so the axon bundle is now central, and the CNS term for a bundle of axons is a tract, making it the optic tract. (Choice B) is incorrect: a ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the PNS, which is wrong on both counts, since the structure is a bundle of axons (not cell bodies) and it lies in the CNS (not the PNS). (Choice C) is incorrect: a nucleus is the CNS location, correctly capturing that the fibers are now central, but it names a cluster of cell bodies rather than a bundle of axons, so it describes the wrong type of structure. (Choice D) is incorrect: nerve is the PNS term for a bundle of axons, which correctly identifies the structure as an axon bundle but wrongly places it in the periphery, whereas beyond the chiasm the fibers are inside the CNS. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the two-variable naming rule (axons versus cell bodies, and CNS versus PNS location) to a bundle that keeps the same fibers but crosses from the peripheral to the central nervous system, rather than recalling a single fixed definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '470fcb67-3017-46f1-96d7-bdb674eac437';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '470fcb67-3017-46f1-96d7-bdb674eac437';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('470fcb67-3017-46f1-96d7-bdb674eac437', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-structure-and-wrong-location'),
  ('470fcb67-3017-46f1-96d7-bdb674eac437', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct-location-wrong-structure'),
  ('470fcb67-3017-46f1-96d7-bdb674eac437', 'D', 'misconception', 'correct-structure-wrong-location');

-- [CNS Tissue Organization] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a study of the central nervous system, researchers destroy one myelinating glial cell in the spinal cord''s white matter and observe that the myelin is lost from segments belonging to several different, physically separate axons at once. Which feature of the affected cell type best accounts for this pattern of injury?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "One oligodendrocyte extends processes that myelinate segments of several separate axons"}, {"label": "B", "text": "One Schwann cell wraps a single axon segment and myelinates no other axons"}, {"label": "C", "text": "One astrocyte contacts many axons to supply the myelin that insulates each of them"}, {"label": "D", "text": "One microglial cell ensheathes multiple neighboring axons to insulate them in myelin"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Myelin in the nervous system is produced by glial cells, and the way each type contacts axons differs between the CNS and the PNS: a single oligodendrocyte sends out multiple processes, each reaching a different axon segment, so one oligodendrocyte can myelinate segments belonging to several separate axons, whereas a single Schwann cell in the PNS wraps only one segment of one axon. Because the injury described spreads myelin loss across multiple separate axons from the death of a single cell, the responsible cell must be the one that services many axons at once. (Choice A) is correct: an oligodendrocyte''s several processes each insulate a different axon segment, so destroying one oligodendrocyte demyelinates parts of several distinct axons simultaneously, exactly the pattern observed. (Choice B) is incorrect: a Schwann cell myelinates only a single segment of a single axon, so its loss would demyelinate just one axon, not several; Schwann cells also reside in the PNS, not the CNS white matter described. (Choice C) is incorrect: astrocytes provide structural and metabolic support and help form the blood-brain barrier, but they do not produce myelin, so they cannot be the source of the myelin lost. (Choice D) is incorrect: microglia act as resident macrophages that clear debris and pathogens in the CNS; they are not myelinating cells and do not ensheathe axons in myelin. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural difference between how oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells contact axons to infer which cell type would produce demyelination across multiple separate axons.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b947bb9c-b362-4e7e-9354-618193ed7390';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b947bb9c-b362-4e7e-9354-618193ed7390';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b947bb9c-b362-4e7e-9354-618193ed7390', 'B', 'partial_truth', 'true Schwann-cell fact (single axon segment, PNS) that does not explain CNS multi-axon demyelination'),
  ('b947bb9c-b362-4e7e-9354-618193ed7390', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributing myelination to astrocytes (support glia)'),
  ('b947bb9c-b362-4e7e-9354-618193ed7390', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributing myelination to microglia (immune glia)');

-- [CNS & Spinal Cord Organization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student jerks her hand away from a hot stove. In this withdrawal reflex, a sensory neuron from the skin triggers a spinal circuit that contracts the biceps brachii to flex the elbow. For the hand to be pulled back quickly, the antagonistic triceps brachii must relax at the same moment. Given that the neuromuscular junction of skeletal muscle is strictly excitatory and skeletal muscle cannot actively relax on command, how does the spinal circuit cause the triceps to relax during this reflex?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "An inhibitory interneuron in the spinal cord hyperpolarizes the motor neuron that supplies the triceps."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The motor neuron supplying the triceps releases an inhibitory transmitter onto the triceps muscle fibers."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The sensory neuron from the skin synapses directly on the triceps muscle fibers to silence them."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The triceps relaxes passively once its own motor neuron stops receiving stretch input."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' In a spinal reflex, sensory input is processed by the spinal cord and converted to motor output; because the neuromuscular junction is strictly excitatory, a motor neuron can only make its muscle contract, and skeletal muscle cannot be actively told to relax. The textbook states that for the antagonist to relax during withdrawal, the motor neuron "needs to ''quiet down,'' or be inhibited," which "occurs through an interneuron in the spinal cord" whose cell body sits in the dorsal horn and which "inhibits the motor neuron that controls the triceps brachii" by "releasing a neurotransmitter or other signal that hyperpolarizes the motor neuron." (Choice A) is correct: relaxation of the antagonist is produced by an inhibitory interneuron in the spinal cord that hyperpolarizes the triceps motor neuron, making it less likely to fire, so the muscle it drives goes slack. (Choice B) is incorrect: motor neurons of skeletal muscle release only excitatory transmitter at the neuromuscular junction and cannot signal a muscle to relax, so an "inhibitory transmitter onto the muscle fibers" contradicts the strictly excitatory nature of the junction. (Choice C) is incorrect: sensory afferents synapse on neurons within the CNS, not on skeletal muscle fibers, so a sensory neuron cannot directly silence a muscle. (Choice D) is incorrect: skeletal muscle does not relax passively on demand within the reflex; the antagonist must be actively unloaded by inhibiting its motor neuron, and stretch input is not what is being withdrawn here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the constraint that the neuromuscular junction is excitatory-only to deduce that antagonist relaxation must come from inhibition of a motor neuron by a spinal interneuron rather than from any direct "relax" signal.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '302dacc1-4950-4f39-94d7-345329b77eba';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '302dacc1-4950-4f39-94d7-345329b77eba';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('302dacc1-4950-4f39-94d7-345329b77eba', 'B', 'misconception', 'motor neurons can send an inhibitory/relax signal to skeletal muscle'),
  ('302dacc1-4950-4f39-94d7-345329b77eba', 'C', 'misconception', 'sensory afferents synapse directly on muscle rather than on CNS neurons'),
  ('302dacc1-4950-4f39-94d7-345329b77eba', 'D', 'misconception', 'skeletal muscle relaxes passively without active inhibition of its motor neuron');

-- [PNS: Somatic vs Autonomic] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A startled person suddenly jumps back and screams when a friend leaps out from behind a corner, all before they have any chance to think about it. Considering how the peripheral nervous system''s functional divisions are defined by the effector tissues they command, which division carried the motor signal that produced this jumping-and-screaming response?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The autonomic nervous system, because the reaction happened involuntarily before any conscious decision was made"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The autonomic nervous system, because a sudden startle triggers a fight-or-flight surge throughout the body"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The somatic nervous system, because the jump and scream are contractions of skeletal muscle"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The enteric nervous system, because it independently coordinates rapid protective movements of the trunk"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' The peripheral nervous system''s functional divisions are distinguished chiefly by the effector tissue each one commands: the somatic nervous system innervates skeletal muscle, while the autonomic nervous system innervates smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. OpenStax states that "voluntary motor response means the contraction of skeletal muscle, but those contractions are not always voluntary," and gives the exact example that being startled and jumping back "is a reflex involving skeletal muscle contractions" — a somatic response that occurs without a conscious decision. (Choice C) is correct: jumping back and screaming are both produced by contractions of skeletal muscle (limb, trunk, and laryngeal muscles), and skeletal muscle is the effector the somatic division commands, so the somatic system carried this motor signal even though the movement was reflexive. (Choice A) is incorrect: lack of conscious control does not by itself make a response autonomic — the text notes that reflexes and habit-learned movements are involuntary yet remain somatic because they act on skeletal muscle; the division is defined by the effector tissue, not by whether a decision was made. (Choice B) is incorrect: although a startle does trigger a sympathetic surge, that autonomic output drives smooth/cardiac muscle and glands (racing heart, sweating), not the skeletal-muscle contractions that constitute the actual jumping and screaming. (Choice D) is incorrect: the enteric nervous system controls the smooth muscle and glandular tissue of the digestive tract, not the skeletal muscles that move the trunk and limbs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the effector-tissue basis of the somatic-versus-autonomic distinction to a reflexive skeletal-muscle response and resist the trap that "involuntary" equals autonomic.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '78094775-f665-45dc-848f-2af5d02bcd3c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '78094775-f665-45dc-848f-2af5d02bcd3c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('78094775-f665-45dc-848f-2af5d02bcd3c', 'A', 'misconception', 'involuntary_means_autonomic'),
  ('78094775-f665-45dc-848f-2af5d02bcd3c', 'B', 'partial_truth', 'correct_side_effect_wrong_effector'),
  ('78094775-f665-45dc-848f-2af5d02bcd3c', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'wrong_division_right_neighborhood');

-- [CNS & Spinal Cord Organization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A patient sustains an injury that cleanly severs the dorsal root of one lumbar spinal nerve; the ventral root of that same nerve is confirmed to be fully intact. A physician then examines the leg region supplied by that spinal nerve. Compared with the patient''s healthy leg, what is the most likely combination of findings in the affected leg?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Loss of skin sensation, an absent knee-jerk reflex, but preserved voluntary muscle contraction"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Loss of skin sensation, an intact knee-jerk reflex, but absent voluntary muscle contraction"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Normal skin sensation, an absent knee-jerk reflex, and absent voluntary muscle contraction"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Loss of skin sensation, an absent knee-jerk reflex, and absent voluntary muscle contraction"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' As a spinal nerve approaches the cord it splits into two roots: the dorsal root carries only the axons of sensory (afferent) neurons, whose cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglion, while the ventral root carries only motor (efferent) axons leaving the cord. A stretch reflex such as the knee-jerk depends on both limbs of a reflex arc: the muscle spindle''s sensory signal enters through the dorsal root and synapses directly on a ventral-horn motor neuron, whose axon exits through the ventral root to contract the muscle. Voluntary contraction, by contrast, is driven by descending motor commands that reach that same ventral-horn motor neuron and leave via the ventral root, without needing the dorsal root at all. (Choice A) is correct: cutting only the dorsal root destroys the afferent limb, so peripheral sensation and the reflex''s sensory input are lost and the knee-jerk disappears; because the ventral root and its motor neurons are intact and still receive descending commands, the patient can still voluntarily contract the muscle. (Choice B) is incorrect: it keeps the knee-jerk reflex and removes voluntary movement, which is backwards, because the reflex requires the severed sensory limb while voluntary contraction runs entirely through the spared ventral (motor) root. (Choice C) is incorrect: it preserves skin sensation, but the dorsal root is the sole entry point for afferent axons from that region, so severing it must abolish sensation. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly loses sensation and the reflex but wrongly abolishes voluntary contraction, reflecting the misconception that a dorsal-root lesion also blocks motor output, when in fact motor axons exit through the untouched ventral root. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the segregated dorsal-sensory/ventral-motor organization of spinal roots and the two-limb structure of a reflex arc to predict which sensory, reflexive, and voluntary functions survive a targeted lesion.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '84ffd452-8468-4a2e-a098-7f08641da4c6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '84ffd452-8468-4a2e-a098-7f08641da4c6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('84ffd452-8468-4a2e-a098-7f08641da4c6', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps which limb of the reflex arc the dorsal root serves'),
  ('84ffd452-8468-4a2e-a098-7f08641da4c6', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes sensation can persist without the dorsal root'),
  ('84ffd452-8468-4a2e-a098-7f08641da4c6', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes any root lesion abolishes both sensation and all motor output');

-- [Repolarization & Hyperpolarization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher records the membrane voltage of a neuron during a single action potential. After the peak, the voltage falls back through -70 mV and continues downward, briefly reaching about -80 mV before slowly returning to the resting value. Which mechanism best accounts for the membrane becoming more negative than the resting potential during this brief interval?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The voltage-gated K+ channels close with a delay, so K+ continues to exit toward its equilibrium value, which lies below -70 mV."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The Na+/K+ pump transiently accelerates, expelling extra Na+ and drawing the membrane below its normal resting value."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The voltage-gated Na+ channels briefly reopen, and the returning Na+ current pulls the membrane past -70 mV."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Voltage-gated Cl- channels open at the peak, and the resulting Cl- influx drives the membrane below -70 mV."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''The Action Potential.'' Repolarization returns the membrane toward the -70 mV resting potential, but the membrane actually overshoots to a more negative value before settling back. The textbook attributes this dip to potassium: K+ reaches electrochemical equilibrium at a voltage below -70 mV, and because the voltage-gated K+ channels are slightly delayed in closing, K+ keeps leaving the cell and drives the membrane below rest until those channels shut and the non-gated channels and Na+/K+ pump restore the resting potential. (Choice A) is correct: the voltage-gated K+ channels lag in closing, so K+ efflux continues toward its equilibrium potential, which sits below -70 mV, pulling the membrane more negative than rest. (Choice B) is incorrect: the Na+/K+ pump works continuously to maintain and restore the resting potential; it does not suddenly speed up to create the undershoot, and its slow, steady activity brings the membrane back up to rest rather than dragging it below. (Choice C) is incorrect: voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated near the peak and Na+ entry depolarizes the membrane (drives it toward positive values), so reopening Na+ channels would move the membrane away from, not past, the negative resting value. (Choice D) is incorrect: although Cl- influx can hyperpolarize a membrane during inhibitory graded potentials, the action potential''s undershoot below rest is produced by continued K+ efflux, not by voltage-gated Cl- channels, which are not the drivers of this phase. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your understanding of ion equilibrium potentials and channel gating kinetics to explain why the membrane transiently drops below its resting value.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7e6b6495-4a8e-45e5-a2d9-aae928949e7c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7e6b6495-4a8e-45e5-a2d9-aae928949e7c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7e6b6495-4a8e-45e5-a2d9-aae928949e7c', 'B', 'misconception', 'pump-causes-hyperpolarization'),
  ('7e6b6495-4a8e-45e5-a2d9-aae928949e7c', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'wrong-ion-direction'),
  ('7e6b6495-4a8e-45e5-a2d9-aae928949e7c', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'chloride-from-IPSP-context');

-- [Impulse Propagation & Conduction Velocity] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher records conduction velocity along two unmyelinated axons that are identical in every respect except caliber: one is wide (large diameter) and one is narrow (small diameter). An identical action potential is triggered at the initial segment of each, and the researcher measures how quickly the depolarization reaches the axon terminal. Which axon conducts the impulse to its terminal faster, and what accounts for the difference?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The narrow axon, because its smaller cytoplasmic volume reaches threshold with less Na+ influx per unit length."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The wide axon, because its larger cross-sectional area lowers the internal resistance opposing the spread of depolarization."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Neither; both conduct at the same velocity, because velocity in unmyelinated axons is fixed by the membrane threshold voltage."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The narrow axon, because a higher surface-area-to-volume ratio packs voltage-gated Na+ channels more densely along its length."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Impulse Propagation & Synapses.'' In an unmyelinated axon, the impulse spreads by continuous conduction: Na+ that enters at one point flows along the inside of the membrane and depolarizes the adjacent segment, opening the next set of voltage-gated Na+ channels. How fast that internal current spreads depends on the resistance the cytoplasm offers to it, and a larger cross-sectional area offers a lower-resistance path. The text states that "Na+-based depolarization spreads faster down a wide axon than down a narrow one. This concept is known as resistance" (A&P 22066–22067). (Choice B) is correct: the wide axon''s greater cross-sectional area presents lower internal (axial) resistance to the spreading Na+ current, so the depolarization travels down it faster, exactly as the wide-river-versus-narrow-creek analogy in the text describes. (Choice A) is incorrect: this reverses the actual relationship, and conduction velocity depends on how fast current spreads down the axon interior, not on how much total Na+ is needed to bring a small volume to threshold—the narrow axon is in fact slower. (Choice C) is incorrect: threshold voltage sets whether an action potential fires, not how fast it propagates; the text explicitly names diameter as a determinant of conduction speed, so velocity is not fixed and geometry matters. (Choice D) is incorrect: in continuous conduction it is the axial resistance to current spread, not channel packing density, that limits speed, and treating a higher surface-area-to-volume ratio as an advantage ignores that the narrow axon actually conducts more slowly. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the resistance principle to a novel two-axon comparison and identify which geometric factor governs the difference in conduction velocity.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8ecd630d-fc4f-4300-b53e-c770dfa23eb2';
