-- [Postsynaptic Receptors & Neurotransmitter Removal] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A patient begins taking a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI), a drug that blocks the presynaptic transporter that normally pumps serotonin back into the serotonergic terminal. At a serotonergic synapse where reuptake is the only clearance route and no cleft enzyme degrades this transmitter, a researcher measures signaling at the postsynaptic receptors after the drug takes effect. Which prediction is correct?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Postsynaptic signaling is prolonged and intensified relative to the untreated synapse."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Postsynaptic signaling returns to its baseline level once the terminal reduces further serotonin release."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Postsynaptic signaling falls sharply because the terminal is starved of serotonin to release."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Postsynaptic signaling switches from excitatory to inhibitory as serotonin builds up in the cleft."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Impulse Propagation & Synapses.'' Serotonin is a biogenic amine whose only route of removal from the synaptic cleft is reuptake: the textbook states that serotonin "is transported back into the presynaptic cell for repackaging" and lists "Reuptake by neurons" as the elimination mechanism for biogenic amines, with no cleft-degrading enzyme (Anatomy & Physiology 2e). Because a neurotransmitter''s effect persists only while it remains bound to its receptors, blocking the sole clearance transporter lets serotonin accumulate and keep re-binding. (Choice A) is correct: with reuptake blocked and no enzyme to substitute, serotonin lingers in the cleft and continues binding its receptors, so the postsynaptic response lasts longer and is stronger than at the untreated synapse. (Choice B) is incorrect: although serotonergic terminals do have autoreceptors, the immediate pharmacological effect of blocking clearance is a net rise in cleft serotonin; presynaptic feedback modulates but does not fully cancel the accumulation, so signaling does not simply return to baseline. (Choice C) is incorrect: it confuses clearance with release—reuptake recycles transmitter for later use, but vesicular release is a separate Ca2+-triggered exocytosis step, and the drug acts fast enough that impaired recycling does not starve the terminal or reduce signaling. (Choice D) is incorrect: accumulating serotonin binds the same receptors more, prolonging and strengthening the existing response; the sign of the effect is set by the receptor type, so simply having more transmitter does not flip an excitatory synapse into an inhibitory one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between a transmitter''s clearance route and its receptor-binding duration to predict the downstream effect of blocking that route.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ea28b186-88de-4b7c-acd2-b0151f88ca0c';
